+\paragraph{Actual quadratic local conditions and completion (Block 22).}
+Continue with the fixed packet $\mathcal P_q$ above. For a
+characteristic-zero field $K$, let
+\[
+j_K:G_K\longrightarrow G_{\Q}
+\]
+be the actual field-induced homomorphism between absolute Galois groups.
+It includes a chosen embedding of algebraic closures. Let $i_K$ be the
+image of the chosen square root $i$ under that embedding. Lean proves
+compatibility with the Galois actions and hence
+\[
+(q\circ j_K)(\sigma)=1
+\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \sigma(i_K)=i_K.
+\]
+The fixed-field theorem for the algebraic closure then gives
+\[
+q\circ j_K=1
+\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
+\exists x\in K,\ x^2=-1.
+\]
+If no such root exists, the local quotient is surjective onto $C_2$.
+These are statements about the actual local Galois map, not a supplied
+arithmetic interpretation of a scalar label.
+
+For every commutative coefficient ring $B$ and $\chi\in\mathcal P_q(B)$,
+define $\chi_K=\chi\circ j_K$. The local character is continuous and
+commutes with coefficient change. The precise local admissibility
+condition is
+\[
+\operatorname{LocallyTrivial}_K(\chi)
+\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \chi_K=1.
+\]
+Descent through the fixed quadratic quotient proves
+\[
+\operatorname{LocallyTrivial}_K(\chi)
+\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
+\bigl(\exists x\in K,\ x^2=-1\bigr)\ \lor\ \chi=1.
+\]
+No reducedness, characteristic-zero or domain hypothesis is imposed on
+$B$. In particular, the formula applies to the finite coefficient
+quotients used in completion.
+
+The p-adic square-root criterion is proved for every prime:
+\[
+\bigl(\exists x\in\Q_p,\ x^2=-1\bigr)
+\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad p\equiv1\pmod4.
+\]
+A root satisfies $\lvert x\rvert_p^2=1$ and is therefore integral.
+Reduction modulo three rules out a root in $\Q_3$, and reduction modulo
+four rules out a root in $\Q_2$. At an odd prime, any residue root of
+$X^2+1$ has nonzero derivative and lifts by the pinned Mathlib Hensel
+theorem. The finite-field square criterion then gives the stated
+congruence. An explicit Hensel application at the residue root $2$ modulo $5$ supplies
+a root in $\Z_5$. Consequently
+\[
+\boxed{
+\operatorname{LocallyTrivial}_{\Q_p}(\chi)
+\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad p\equiv1\pmod4\ \lor\ \chi=1.
+}
+\]
+The arithmetic prime $p$ is independent of the coefficient prime. For
+the two integral $\Z_2$-valued characters, the sign character is locally
+trivial exactly at primes congruent to one modulo four. Both distinct
+global characters are locally trivial at five, while the sign character
+is nontrivial on the full local Galois groups at three and two.
+
+The inertia theorem uses actual residue actions. For a valuation subring
+$A$ of an extension field $L/K$, let $D_A$ be its decomposition subgroup
+in $\operatorname{Aut}_K(L)$ and put
+\[
+I_A=\ker\bigl(D_A\longrightarrow\operatorname{Aut}(k_A)\bigr),
+\]
+where $k_A$ is the residue field. If $2$ is a unit in $A$, every member
+of $I_A$ fixes every $y\in L$ with $y^2=-1$. Indeed $y^{-1}=-y$, so
+$y$ belongs to $A$ and is a unit there. An automorphism sends $y$ to
+$y$ or $-y$. Their residues are distinct because $2$ and $y$ are units,
+whereas inertia fixes residues. The second possibility is excluded.
+
+For every prime $p$, Lean constructs a valuation subring
+$A\subseteq\overline{\Q_p}$ containing the integral image of $\Z_p$
+such that the induced map $\Z_p\to A$ is local. For odd $p$, two is
+a unit. The preceding argument applies to every valuation subring
+containing that integral image, and hence every member of
+$\mathcal P_q(B)$ is trivial on its inertia subgroup after actual local
+restriction. The existence theorem supplies a locally dominating choice,
+so this assertion is not vacuous.
+
+This inertia is the actual residue-action kernel inside $D_A$. No equality
+$D_A=G_{\Q_p}$ is established in this block. Nor is ramification at two
+proved: the nontrivial full local character there is a separate result.
+At three the $\Z_2$-valued sign character is nontrivial on the full local
+Galois group but trivial on every inertia subgroup covered by the theorem.
+Thus local triviality and the stated inertia triviality are distinguished
+by checked arithmetic examples.
+
+The local conditions survive coefficient completion by a proved theorem.
+For an $I$-adically separated commutative ring $R$,
+\[
+\operatorname{LocallyTrivial}_K(\chi)
+\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
+\operatorname{LocallyTrivial}_K(\chi\bmod I^m)
+\text{ for every }m\ge1.
+\]
+If $-1$ has a square root in $K$, every packet member satisfies the
+condition. Otherwise the condition says that the character is globally
+trivial, and separatedness detects that equality through all reductions.
+This proves the closure statement for the independently defined arithmetic
+property.
+
+Suppose now that $R$ is $I$-adically complete and that every positive
+quotient $R/I^m$ is finite. Let $C_m\subseteq\mathcal P_q(R/I^m)$ be
+allowed subsets preserved by reduction, and let $(p_s)_{s\in S}$ be any
+indexed family of arithmetic primes. The index set $S$ need not be finite.
+Lean proves the simultaneous arithmetic completion criterion
+\[
+\begin{split}
+&\exists\chi\in\mathcal P_q(R),\quad
+ \chi\text{ continuous},\quad
+ \chi\bmod I^m\in C_m\ (m\ge1),\quad
+ \chi_{\Q_{p_s}}=1\ (s\in S)\\
+&\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
+ \forall m\ge1,\ \exists\psi\in C_m,\quad
+ \forall s\in S,\ p_s\equiv1\pmod4\ \lor\ \psi=1.
+\end{split}
+\]
+The constructed character also satisfies every odd-prime valuation-inertia
+condition stated above. Finite-branch compactness selects all finite
+characters together; no coherent branch or surjective transition maps
+are assumed. The new local-closure implication is proved, not supplied.
+Every-level occupancy remains a substantive hypothesis. One nonsplit
+selected prime forces the completed global character to be trivial.
+
+For the integral dyadic packet, a single detecting precision is also
+proved: reduction modulo four detects local triviality uniformly in the
+arithmetic prime. Modulo two does not suffice. The sign character is
+nontrivial locally at three, while its reduction modulo two is trivial.
+This is a coefficient-depth failure, distinct from splitting of the
+arithmetic field.
+
+Block 22 finishes this actual local admissibility and completion step for
+the fixed quadratic packet. It does not identify all packets in the
+manuscript's common arithmetic spine, attach Weil--Deligne objects, or
+prove coefficient and Hodge--Tate/period comparisons. The decomposition
+and full local Galois group identification remains a separate inertia
+obligation, and ramification at two is not claimed. The full arithmetic
+ledger remains fifteen checked source claims and five partial claims.
+Universal occupancy and natural finite seed rank remain conjectures.
